    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: '99 (trick-taking)',
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Ninety-Nine: trick-taking with exact-trick bidding. Bid by discarding 3 cards (Clubs=3, Hearts=2, Spades=1, Diamonds=0). 3-4 players; 36 or 52 cards. Follow suit; score for making exact bid and premiums for face-up or revealed hand. Designed by David Parlett 1967-68.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Invented by David Parlett (1967-68). Trick-taking with exact-trick bidding via discard (suit = trick value). Distinct from adding game 99.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Minimum Players: 2\nMaximum Players: 6\nOptimal Players: 4\nPartnerships: Optional',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Type: Standard 52-card deck\nNumber of Decks: 1\nJokers: Not included',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard 52-card deck\nScore sheet (optional)\nTable space',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Cards per Player: Varies by player count\nDealing Pattern: Clockwise\n--',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'Ninety-Nine for three players (standard game): Base Ninety-Nine uses a 36-card French-suited pack, deals 12 cards to each of three players, and has everyone discard three bid cards whose suits encode an exact trick target.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Ninety-Nine for four players: Four-player Ninety-Nine uses a full 52-card deck, deals 13 cards each, and allows bids up to 10 tricks, with three diamonds representing either 0 or 10 tricks.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Ninety-Nine for five players: Five-player Ninety-Nine uses a Five Hundred style pack including elevens and twelves, with each player discarding three bid cards to aim for up to nine tricks.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Two-player Ninety-Nine with dummy: In the two-player variant a third dummy hand is dealt, its three bid cards are set aside unseen, and the live players both bid and may declare while also playing cards from the dummy hand.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Original Ninety-Nine with joker: The original three-player version adds a joker to the 36-card pack, uses the turn-up card to set trumps, and plays a fixed rubber of nine deals.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Declarer chooses trumps: In this variation a player who declares or reveals also chooses the trump suit for the deal instead of using the usual success-based trump rotation.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Unbidden Ninety-Nine: Unbidden Ninety-Nine delays bidding until the final three cards of each hand, using those cards as the bid while players have already played nine tricks.',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
